# Repair plan for failing-set

## Diagnosis

The failing set is two Go tests in one package, `tests/suite/calc_test.go`
(package `suite_test`, module `fixture/buildcodefix`):

- `TestA` asserts `calc.A() != 1` is false — i.e. `calc.A()` must return `1`.
- `TestB` asserts `calc.B() != 2` is false — i.e. `calc.B()` must return `2`.

Both fail with the same marker `SHARED-FAILURE-EVIDENCE`, which is why they
are reported as one failure group; the group's quoted evidence only shows
`TestA` because `t.Fatal` aborts that test first, but `TestB` is failing for
the identical reason.

The production source under the declared edit root is
`services/calc/calc.go`:

```
package calc
func A() int{return 0}
func B() int{return 0}
```

Both exported functions return the zero value, so neither assertion can hold.
This is a production defect, not a test defect: per `CLAUDE.md` and
`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`, `services/calc` is the only writable
tree and the failing assertions are the specification.

There is no hidden dependency, build tag, interface, or wiring problem — the
test imports `fixture/buildcodefix/services/calc` directly, the module path in
`go.mod` matches, and the package compiles today (the tests run and fail on
the assertion rather than on a build error). So the repair is purely the
return-value contract of the two functions.

## What to change

### Part 1 — `services/calc/calc.go`: correct `A()`

Serves: `/go-test/go-test:fixture.buildcodefix.tests.suite::TestA`

Change `A()` so that it returns the integer `1` instead of `0`. Keep the
exported name, the `package calc` declaration, the zero-argument signature
and the `int` result type exactly as they are — the test calls `calc.A()` with
no arguments and compares the result to an untyped integer constant, so the
signature must not change.

### Part 2 — `services/calc/calc.go`: correct `B()`

Serves: `/go-test/go-test:fixture.buildcodefix.tests.suite::TestB`

Change `B()` so that it returns the integer `2` instead of `0`. Same
constraints as Part 1: keep the name exported, keep the no-argument signature
and the single `int` return.

Both parts are edits to the same single file; no new files, no new packages,
no changes to `go.mod` and no new imports are required.

